\description{
Resolves a selected public random-effect quantity to the exact scalar
dependence of its marginal covariance contribution. The result is compiled
exclusively from the fitted parameter map and formula random-effect
metadata; posterior draws are neither inspected nor used to infer an update
form. An unsupported plan can include a \code{conditional} entry containing
\code{required_active} and \code{plan}. The nested plan applies only to posterior rows
where every named inclusion indicator equals one. It does not declare an
unconditional update for the selected public quantity.
}
\details{
\code{family = "affine"} means that, conditional on every non-selected posterior
coordinate, the row covariance has the exact form \code{A + h(value) * B}, where
\code{value} is the coordinate declared by \code{coefficient_input} (\code{"source"} or
\code{"quantity"}) and \code{h} is recorded in \code{coefficient_transform}.
\code{family = "factor"} identifies an exact candidate-dependent covariance
factor, while \code{family = "markov"} identifies a compiled structured Markov
state. Allocation-derived component SDs are affine on every covariance
structure when the allocation scale drives all columns of every block it
reaches (\verb{A + h(v) B} with \code{h} the square of the SD or the identity of the
variance, \code{B} the summed covariance of \code{blocks} per unit of \code{h}); they are
factor \code{column_scale} updates only when it drives part of the columns of the
one block it reaches. All of them hold the allocation weights fixed and scale
every driven column SD by the same factor (\code{held_fixed}, above), so the
\code{column_scale} plan of an allocation-derived SD differs from that of a direct
SD, which replaces the scale of its own columns only (one column, or every
column in \code{driven_columns} for an SD shared by several columns). Their
\code{coefficient_scale_transform} converts a selected variance to its square
root and leaves a selected SD unchanged. These
quantity-scale plans require the public SD to be on the fitted coefficient
scale; after formula scaling that changes it, the plan is unsupported.
Unsupported plans carry a structural reason. The accessor
never estimates affineness from evaluated covariance matrices.

The invariant covariance of a block with several coefficient columns is a
dense rows x rows matrix of the fitted data. Its estimated peak memory is
checked against the \code{BayesTools.random_effects_memory_limit_bytes} option
(see \link{BayesTools}) before it is formed; above the ceiling the accessor stops
with an error of class \code{BayesTools_random_effects_memory_limit} (also
\code{BayesTools_random_effects_error}).

The \code{conditional} entry of an unsupported plan is compiled only for a
variance allocation with exactly two single-column block targets, a scalar
total-variance source and inclusion gates on the blocks; an allocation with
more than two targets has no conditional plan and stays unsupported.
